\\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634602 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ca310c-8e17-5cce-81e6-8f4499c54cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186633856} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb6c980-8101-5640-91b5-012a01ff1fc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186633881} {:answer/id #uuid "6bdb9c12-8a64-5658-ad82-5ff8a91da558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634069} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad1b22c-dded-5e4e-beba-67a876c37128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634146} {:answer/id #uuid "cd130a32-0f5f-5824-8eaf-cf4afd4c8bb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634305}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634305} :answer-field/id #uuid "558ba427-0f7c-5e07-8fd5-9624ff3aa314" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4804f949-60e2-54ee-a41f-a3de4c7e62b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-104086" :question/problem "The vector equation of a straight line is given by\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the vector equation of the line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The general form of the vector equation for a line is\n\n$$\nr=p+vt,\n$$\n\nand comparing this general form to our equation above, we find that\n\n- $p=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$ is the position vector of the point $P(-5,7,8)$ that lies on the line, and\n\n- the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ is parallel to the line.\n\nTherefore, the cross product equation for the line is\n\n$$\n(r-\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b0cb3e9-16de-5e02-bf36-58e73c3818d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{-8}=\\frac{y-10}{2}=\\frac{z+8}{-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186633985} {:answer/id #uuid "4bac04ea-702f-5460-b72e-61a7c857e4ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+8}{2}=\\frac{y+9}{2}=\\frac{z-10}{-8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186633990} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd3cb33-a0e7-594a-8c18-6c5eb1228042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+8}{2}=\\frac{y-2}{10}=\\frac{z+9}{-8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634033} {:answer/id #uuid "938d6acc-907e-51ea-9f30-b5130dee380b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{y}{10}=\\frac{z}{-8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634164} {:answer/id #uuid "c015b2f8-141a-5590-a4d7-759a20076050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{-8}=\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634264}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634033} :answer-field/id #uuid "048a403b-baf2-52e2-b6b1-79902583befd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "623654fc-7c2e-59aa-ba8c-88b868131323" :question/math-academy-id "q-104088" :question/problem "Write the vector equation of the line\n\n$$\n(r-\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nin canonical form." :question/worked-solution "The equation of the line using the cross product takes the general form\n\n$$\n(r-p)\\times v=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this general form to the given equation, we find that\n\n- the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}$ is parallel to the line, and\n\n- $p=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}$ is the position vector of the point $P(-8,2,-9)$ that lies on the line.\n\nNow, the сanonical equation of the straight line passing through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-8)}{2}=\\frac{y-2}{10}=\\frac{z-(-9)}{-8}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+8}{2}=\\frac{y-2}{10}=\\frac{z+9}{-8}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634626 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2867a12c-a03f-572e-808c-3cceab4687e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186633896} {:answer/id #uuid "2d35fa18-0f28-5d7c-8ef9-d59a70563017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186633907} {:answer/id #uuid "484c180f-c6ad-5a61-b777-9954bea9b6c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186633979} {:answer/id #uuid "60928f7c-48a5-5741-a17e-ab1ea098c335" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634041} {:answer/id #uuid "f11d64b1-386a-5204-aeb0-dc61bf49c62c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634384}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186633979} :answer-field/id #uuid "075a4df5-88ef-5709-a41f-b5e9df2c9d65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6e4853de-c86d-5d92-bc90-627fc3dfe4cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-104085" :question/problem "The vector equation of a straight line is given by \n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the vector equation of the line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The general form of the vector equation for a line is\n\n$$\nr=p+vt,\n$$\n\nand comparing this general form to our equation above, we find that\n\n- $p=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ is the position vector of the point $P(2,0,5)$ that lies on the line, and\n\n- the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}$ is parallel to the line.\n\nTherefore, the cross product equation for the line is\n\n$$\n(r-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d14aa23-fbda-5439-a7fd-f0d6b2c2a234" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186633833} {:answer/id #uuid "1c8f2d9c-372e-573f-8434-05714cae2ca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186633868} {:answer/id #uuid "3b03bf04-3694-5547-9c63-8becf7966044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186633939} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9feb68-9140-5487-aa58-2e9f6776f147" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634304} {:answer/id #uuid "d096e129-d4b1-5437-935c-61169337a017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634313}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186633833} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1d3259c-f445-552f-a0e4-3283598f7886" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7afdd23f-761c-5fe3-953b-de8f549d37ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-72127" :question/problem "A line passes through the points $P(1,-1,-4)$ and $Q(2,7,-3).$ Find its vector equation in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points $P(1,-1,-4)$ and $Q(2,7,-3).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =q-p \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to get the equation of the line using the cross product, we substitute in our values of $p$ and $v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n(r-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634651 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "169ec46d-1dcc-5d8a-8a55-a58f9158a7f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=t\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186633855} {:answer/id #uuid "af9c36b4-46bf-54d7-bbf6-b4d2be9d2513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=t\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634223} {:answer/id #uuid "b2add294-b064-5b4c-a008-d693a95e8e42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634231} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a48721-98fd-5e4b-8f54-46e5ab2d23dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634245} {:answer/id #uuid "c0df6f6d-db83-5c2d-95a1-3e5c2c828c83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634267}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634231} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bb2f648-1c09-555e-a3e5-a9287ff3d236" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9b79eafa-60de-5bf6-a918-21703a59f916" :question/math-academy-id "q-72081" :question/problem "Write the vector equation of the line\n\n$$\n(r-\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nin the form $r=p+tv$, $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the line using the cross product takes the general form\n\n$$\n(r-p)\\times v=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this general form to the given equation, we find that\n\n- the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}$ is parallel to the line, and\n\n- $p=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ is the position vector of the point $P(-5,4,-4)$ that lies on the line.\n\nTherefore, the given vector equation can be written as\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634656 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "273e7d48-0415-58f7-ac31-017fbe0983b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186633894} {:answer/id #uuid "392251ff-5d79-5dd2-aa15-552bc721e83b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186633934} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb4af4e-fd57-5841-b175-2b8cf0c50be7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186633943} {:answer/id #uuid "be8a82f2-6580-56de-a0b8-bfdc61939ab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634259} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc06144-73d2-5c71-8dd8-9c254588f47c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634407}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186633894} :answer-field/id #uuid "56b847f7-358e-58fd-99ac-9050622732d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a84f331f-52fe-55a2-a0dd-1517356e80da" :question/math-academy-id "q-33615" :question/problem "The canonical equation of the line is given by \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-2}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{-2}.\n$$\nFind the vector equation of this line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Looking at the denominators of the canonical equation, we find that the direction vector is $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe also have the position vector $p=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ for the point $P(2,2,1)$ that lies on the line. \n\nTherefore, the equation can be written as\n\n$$\n(r-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634660 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d491ab-a27b-59ec-a2b8-a5f554a4d549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1+3t \\\\ y &=1-5t \\\\ z &=1+4t\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186633815} {:answer/id #uuid "12d44946-d2b6-54b2-822a-65a8214e3eb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3+t \\\\ y &=-5+t \\\\ z &=4+t\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186633848} {:answer/id #uuid "5b00fab6-22fb-5a3e-8a35-c3b6e94c28bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1+t \\\\ y &=1+t \\\\ z &=1+t\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634023} {:answer/id #uuid "85d2be6a-12af-582e-b0b3-12aaedb9e171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3t \\\\ y &=4t \\\\ z &=5t\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634134} {:answer/id #uuid "b763261f-ba6a-53a9-8c4e-4bf0a93290b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=t \\\\ y &=t \\\\ z &=t\\end{aligned}\\right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634241}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186633815} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d926505-4eec-5ca3-bd05-a394524a26ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ae974453-c8ad-52e1-84ac-c87742d2b7a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-33626" :question/problem "The vector equation of a line is given by \n\n$$\n(r-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=0.\n$$\n\nFind its parametric equations." :question/worked-solution "The equation of the line using the cross product takes the general form\n\n$$\n(r-p)\\times v=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this general form to the given equation, we find that\n\n- the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ is parallel to the line, and\n\n- $p=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is the position vector of the point $P(1,1,1)$ that lies on the line.\n\nTherefore, the given vector equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3t \\\\ -5t \\\\ 4t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+3t \\\\ 1-5t \\\\ 1+4t\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand finally, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1+3t \\\\ y &=1-5t \\\\ z &=1+4t\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634663 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a393f5f-31d8-51e8-8864-629a177aab24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186633898} {:answer/id #uuid "7708ea0d-4761-54bf-a55a-a89a166561ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634092} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd9d6ca-5967-5945-9547-da61ed212bcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634106} {:answer/id #uuid "b377dcac-2444-5c7c-916e-ddf3a7b2ba11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634233} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe4805b-7974-516b-b492-c4794782cd28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634344}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634092} :answer-field/id #uuid "82f312e0-f223-5ba3-841a-18ef6227858f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b1e2fcd1-96f9-59a0-b80a-3391322975e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-33616" :question/problem "The canonical equation of a line is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x+3}{-4}=\\frac{y+1}{5}=\\frac{z-2}{-3}.\n$$\n\nFind the vector equation of this line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Looking at the denominators of the canonical equation, we find that the direction vector is $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe also have the position vector $p=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ for the point $P(-3,-1,2)$ that lies on the line. \n\nTherefore, the equation can be written as\n\n$$\n(r-\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634672 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bd61b37-af91-52f3-9c3c-8b22c08a3ecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634027} {:answer/id #uuid "5c476691-e696-5a76-9739-7afc17d01de4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634028} {:answer/id #uuid "6bbb0618-516f-5d2b-88c7-38fe66ce6fb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634068} {:answer/id #uuid "f50ea8f0-44bb-5baf-87f0-0163405e2d16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634394} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2e551c-4ba7-5c2c-80b2-72d4d5185551" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634412}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634068} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f822fcb-c3e3-5bc1-af04-e714cafc4d9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bf29f0b1-7ad5-5266-998d-ece9cf13873e" :question/math-academy-id "q-72118" :question/problem "The vector equation of a straight line is given by\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the vector equation of the line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The general form of the vector equation for a line is\n\n$$\nr=p+vt,\n$$\n\nand comparing this general form to our equation above, we find that\n\n- $p=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}$ is the position vector of the point $P(8,7,-9)$ that lies on the line, and\n\n- the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$ is parallel to the line.\n\nTherefore, the cross product equation for the line is\n\n$$\n(r-\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1675e303-2293-56fc-8739-5b7fbe65597b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186633854} {:answer/id #uuid "622693e1-2d17-5abf-975f-dc16ab361e91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634045} {:answer/id #uuid "6459d24b-856e-52dc-a691-9375fbf48c74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634050} {:answer/id #uuid "ad416708-32be-567c-b404-8680f682e21b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634221} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a0659b-5c4c-50a8-ac95-5c9714c7a240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634228}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634050} :answer-field/id #uuid "53213467-b0de-550c-a07e-cbde8f1d0522" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "da6a6fc0-cdd3-5bc7-9b13-eb3947bd5307" :question/math-academy-id "q-32926" :question/problem "The parametric equations of a straight line are given by \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3t \\\\ y &=-1+5t \\\\ z &=7-3t,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the vector equation of the line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The corresponding vector equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3t \\\\ -1+5t \\\\ 7-3t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3t \\\\ 5t \\\\ -3t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe general form of the vector equation for a line is\n\n$$\nr=p+vt,\n$$\n\nand comparing this general form to our equation above, we find that\n\n- $p=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$ is the position vector of the point $P(0,-1,7)$ that lies on the line, and\n\n- the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ is parallel to the line.\n\nTherefore, the cross product equation for the line is\n\n$$\n(r-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634689 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45b7e9d1-62b6-5427-9720-40cc5d89468c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186633970} {:answer/id #uuid "cbdd244f-9db9-532c-bca9-469ef05c3179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634303} {:answer/id #uuid "db0cd00c-244f-5f70-9e41-a8713d3833a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634341} {:answer/id #uuid "f3db6488-977a-5a6c-be33-e61fa983905e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634391} {:answer/id #uuid "f51a1e57-bc61-52a4-b81e-44874a9dce96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634395}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634391} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3871aab-41a7-549e-8510-106459c7525b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ebe73d1d-acf1-5df1-92f6-70778adc30e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-71996" :question/problem "The canonical equation of a line is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x+2}{-4}=\\frac{y+8}{3}=\\frac{z-6}{-9}.\n$$\nFind the vector equation of this line in the form $(r-p)\\times v=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Looking at the denominators of the canonical equation, we find that the direction vector is $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe also have the position vector $p=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ for the point $PP(-2,-8,6)$ that lies on the line. \n\nTherefore, the equation can be written as\n\n$$\n(r-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix})\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186635479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02e99ad7-fea9-50f9-a88d-af33e0df3c03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634833} {:answer/id #uuid "3181fbd9-e9af-5314-96b2-fdf02148b1c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634920} {:answer/id #uuid "45faff33-9e8b-5107-a682-a5b3eccb3894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634965} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3f0373-762e-5bae-8d4f-e0ef04441b01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186634990} {:answer/id #uuid "8202c44f-e9b3-5170-9113-e060e8509f9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{15}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186635091}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186634833} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a1883b0-a6dd-5419-a5cb-1c797c05d892" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186635373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "312fb291-37eb-5a5b-ae9d-c18540acd3c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-110710" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the planes ${Π}_{1}$ and ${Π}_{2}$ with equations $(r-\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=0$  and \n$(r-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=0$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "To compute the angle between the planes, we compute the angle between the corresponding normal vectors.\n\n- The equation of the plane ${Π}_{1}$ is given in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0,$ so the normal vector is ${n}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,.$\n\n- The equation of the plane ${Π}_{2}$ is also given in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0,$ so the normal vector is ${n}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{{n}_{1}\\cdot {n}_{2}}{|{n}_{1}||{n}_{2}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 5+1\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{50}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{10\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the principal value, we get \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)={30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acute angle between the planes ${Π}_{1}$ and ${Π}_{2}$ is ${30}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186636448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15bb2a05-5ad7-5efc-b820-ed2daf994243" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186635803} {:answer/id #uuid "1bac9676-8630-5dfd-94a9-cd92e0d87aef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186635813} {:answer/id #uuid "a091fded-0982-5a6e-b942-c4b023f1f8aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186636064} {:answer/id #uuid "d18d34bf-2e36-53b4-b9a7-6afebe34ba4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186636162} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d0c4c4-a104-5d15-b5ef-56da788668d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186636261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186635803} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4df480e-40f8-5832-990e-4f59918e607e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186636400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3d98cbe6-83e7-5fe0-a160-4c560d5542ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-33458" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the planes ${Π}_{1}$ and ${Π}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$$\n(r-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,2x-4y-2z=8.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to check if the planes are parallel. The normal vectors of ${Π}_{1}$ and ${Π}_{2}$ are, respectively, \n\n$\\,$ ${n}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{n}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince ${n}_{2}=-2{n}_{1},$ the planes are parallel.\n\nNow, let's pick the point $P(0,-1,0)$ that lies on ${Π}_{1}.$ To pick a point that lies on ${Π}_{2},$ we assign some values to $x$ and $y,$ and solve the equation of the plane for $z.$ Setting $x=1$ and $y=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4y-2z & =8 \\\\ 2(1)-4(-1)-2z & =8 \\\\ -2z & =8-6 \\\\ z & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point  $Q(1,-1,-1)$ lies on ${Π}_{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩-⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe shortest distance $d$ between the planes is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of any of the planes. Therefore, we calculate the distance $d$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{{n}_{1}}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot {n}_{1}|}{|{n}_{1}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|1\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 1|}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-2|}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186636451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05bfa94e-fc00-583c-b973-f45886e97caf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186635767} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea4d679-a236-5fa3-9574-527997674bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186636061} {:answer/id #uuid "b76be07f-973c-5559-983a-bf7f3b86e56f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{11}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186636102} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f3d79c-48da-5082-a31d-e4975c68b8e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186636237} {:answer/id #uuid "f9cddf9f-c8a3-57df-bec1-90a8c643435b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{11}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186636267}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186636061} :answer-field/id #uuid "49b0885c-d20a-5789-94bb-d21318dab63c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186636322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3fba3d41-f601-5a5e-8b77-818204337d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-112914" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the planes ${Π}_{1}$ and ${Π}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$$\n(r-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(r-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to check if the planes are parallel. The normal vectors of ${Π}_{1}$ and ${Π}_{2}$ are, respectively, \n\n$\\,$ ${n}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{n}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}∦{n}_{2},$ the planes are not parallel. Therefore, they must intersect each other. This means that the shortest distance between the planes is zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186636458 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "182bb3fd-09aa-58cc-b473-ab8fc0948437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186635805} {:answer/id #uuid "1e8a263d-981e-5915-9add-48731aa5fc7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186635822} {:answer/id #uuid "73a763e8-a684-5959-9208-6a357047ce09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186635998} {:answer/id #uuid "c279d197-0d7e-5152-b225-17c50944a490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186636132} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d053c0-0c43-5242-903d-16cbc266cf70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186636175}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186635805} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef89e0ee-0b12-5018-b2c5-48eabe8391e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186636411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "487c09b8-1e36-5f35-a357-a8afeef5b9e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-90627" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the planes ${Π}_{1}$ and ${Π}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$$\n(r-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,x-2y+z=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to check if the planes are parallel. The normal vectors of ${Π}_{1}$ and ${Π}_{2}$ are, respectively, \n\n$\\,$ ${n}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{n}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}=-2{n}_{2},$ the planes are parallel.\n\nNow, let's pick the point $P(-2,-1,4)$ that lies on ${Π}_{1}.$ To pick a point that lies on ${Π}_{2},$ we assign some values to $x$ and $y,$ and solve the equation of the plane for $z.$ Setting $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+z & =0 \\\\ (1)-2(0)+z & =0 \\\\ z & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $Q(1,0,-1)$ lies on ${Π}_{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,0,-1⟩-⟨-2,-1,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,1,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe shortest distance $d$ between the planes is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of any of the planes. Therefore, we calculate the distance $d$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{{n}_{2}}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot {n}_{2}|}{|{n}_{2}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)+(-5)\\cdot 1|}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-4|}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
